            <ArticleTitle>Procedures for Estimating Magnitude of Effects.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>151-161</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/00223980.1973.9915603</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A review is provided of methods that estimate the magnitude of effects within experimental designs. Intraclass correlation type procedures are appropriate with any ANOVA model. Friedman's r m and Cohen's power analysis apply to many non-ANOVA type situations, but only to the Fixed Effects ANOVA design. The Gaito utility procedure has two advantages over other intraclass correlation type measures: the coefficients sum to unity (or to 100%), and coefficients for error components are obtained.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
